# Proposed Firestore rules change — NOT DEPLOYED

Base: firestore.rules on main at be99143d1f7d9351ef8c9b69cf27aa489e490332.
Status: proposal only. The deployed/source firestore.rules file is intentionally unchanged.

## Unified diff

    --- a/firestore.rules
    +++ b/firestore.rules
    @@
    +   // Meeting working documents, reviews and attendance signatures are mediated
    +   // by authenticated Cloudflare Worker routes. The Worker verifies the Firebase
    +   // ID token, checks meeting/participant authority, and writes audit records
    +   // with its server identity. Clients must not bypass those checks via Firestore.
    +   match /meetingWorkingDocuments/{id} {
    +     allow read, create, update, delete: if false;
    +   }
    +   match /meetingDocumentReviews/{id} {
    +     allow read, create, update, delete: if false;
    +   }
    +   match /meetingAttendanceSignatures/{id} {
    +     allow read, create, update, delete: if false;
    +   }
        match /{document=**}{allow read,write:if false;}

## Reasons

1. These records are intended to be read/written through authenticated Worker endpoints, not directly from browser clients.
2. The Worker applies meeting-specific host/secretary/participant checks and records audit events. Direct client writes would bypass that application-level validation.
3. Explicit deny rules document the intended security boundary. Firestore combines matching allow expressions with OR semantics, so these explicit denies do not override a separate matching allow; the existing catch-all deny remains in place.
4. Before approval, verify no existing client code reads these collections directly and run the Firestore rules unit tests. If a future approved design needs direct client reads, add narrowly scoped role/meeting membership predicates rather than broad authenticated-user access.

No rules were deployed or modified by this proposal.

## Additional proposed tightening — meeting reads

Reason: the current `/meetings/{id}` rule allows any authenticated account to read every meeting. The existing `meetingCategoryReadAllowed(resource.data)` helper checks administrator status, initiator/participant membership, and authorized roles for restricted meeting categories. Reusing that helper narrows the read boundary without inventing a second role policy.

Review risk: list/query behavior may change for accounts that previously relied on broad signed-in access. Before approval, run Firestore Rules unit tests and query-shape checks for all meeting categories and role types. This is a proposal only; do not deploy without separate approval.

```diff
--- a/firestore.rules
+++ b/firestore.rules
@@ -374,1 +374,1 @@
-  match /meetings/{id}{allow read:if signedIn();allow create:if (meetingRegistrar()&&request.resource.data.get("initiatorUid","")==request.auth.uid&&validMeetingCategory(request.resource.data))&&actualCreateAllowed();allow update:if (admin()||completeInitialMeetingRegistration()||meetingSessionUpdateAllowed(resource.data))&&actualUpdateAllowed();allow delete:if (admin())&&actualDeleteAllowed();}
+  match /meetings/{id}{allow read:if meetingCategoryReadAllowed(resource.data);allow create:if (meetingRegistrar()&&request.resource.data.get("initiatorUid","")==request.auth.uid&&validMeetingCategory(request.resource.data))&&actualCreateAllowed();allow update:if (admin()||completeInitialMeetingRegistration()||meetingSessionUpdateAllowed(resource.data))&&actualUpdateAllowed();allow delete:if (admin())&&actualDeleteAllowed();}
```
